urns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"**No, APs aren't paid by the sponsor, and no, they aren't obligated to create or redeem.**\n\n- **Compensation:** Authorized Participants (typically large broker-dealers) sign an AP Agreement with the fund's distributor. They **pay** the fund a creation/redemption transaction fee. They earn money through **arbitrage**, buying the cheaper of the ETF or its basket and selling the richer one, and from client facilitation and market-making spreads. Separately, some exchanges pay incentives to lead market makers, but that is not AP compensation from the sponsor.\n\n- **Obligation:** The agreement gives APs the **right**, not the duty, to transact in creation units. They can step back at any time, as some did briefly in stressed fixed-income markets in March 2020.\n\n- **Mitigant:** Most ETFs have several APs, and other APs or market makers can step in. This is why the number of active APs matters for an ETF's resilience.","ttft_ms":2986,"type":"result","duration_ms":6203,"uuid":"f319b8df-441f-4283-a731-62fe8b6a133f","ttft_stream_ms":1417,"time_to_request_ms":72,"first_content_frame_ms":1417,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
